---

For a quick, self-signed setup:

   1. Create a directory for your certificate files:

```bash
mkdir ~/silo-certs
cd ~/silo-certs
```

2. Create an OpenSSL configuration file (san.cnf) with the required Subject Alternative Names (SANs). Replace silo1.sys.example.com with your silo’s actual FQDN:

san.cnf

```ini
[req]
default_bits  = 2048
distinguished_name = req_distinguished_name
req_extensions = req_ext
x509_extensions = v3_req
prompt = no
[req_distinguished_name]
countryName = US
stateOrProvinceName = N/A
localityName = N/A
organizationName = Self-signed certificate
commonName = silo1.sys.example.com
[req_ext]
subjectAltName = @alt_names
[v3_req]
subjectAltName = @alt_names
[alt_names]
DNS.1 = silo1.sys.example.com
```

3. Generate the self-signed certificate:

```bash
openssl req -x509 -nodes -days 365 -newkey rsa:2048 -keyout key.pem -out cert.pem -config san.cnf
```

4. Verify the generated certificate:

```bash
openssl x509 -in cert.pem -text -noout
```

